+   * The HPS f2sdram POSTS writes (mem_shim has no writeresponsevalid / commit ack), so a vbuf
+   * READ issued just behind the WRITE frontier can return DDR data whose write has not yet
+   * committed -> the decoder gets STALE ring content -> a mid-frame decode desync that JITTERS
+   * run-to-run with DDR latency (refresh / ARM contention). It is invisible in Verilator
+   * (fixed-latency modeled DDR = deterministic ordering) and at a /dev/mem dump (writes long since
+   * landed). The race is worst at decode START, when the ring is nearly empty so the read frontier
+   * sits right on the write frontier — which is exactly why the desync lands in the top MB rows and
+   * its row jitters [observed 1,6,7,10] across reloads of a BYTE-PERFECT bitstream (write path
+   * confirmed 100% intact via vbuf DDR dump).
